===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{m|non|auga}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{m|gem-pro|*augô}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|is}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₃ekʷ-||eye; to see}}. Cognates include Latin {{m|la|oculus}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|अक्षि|sc=Deva|tr=ákṣi}}, Gothic {{m|got|sc=Goth|𐌰𐌿𐌲𐍉}}, Old English {{m|ang|ēaġe}}, English {{m|en|eye}}, Scots {{m|sco|ee}}, Swedish {{m|sv|öga}}, Danish {{m|da|øje}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈøyːɣa]|lang=is}}
* {{audio|is-auga.ogg|Audio|lang=is}}
* {{rhymes|øyːɣa|lang=is}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|n|auga|augu}}

# [[eye]]
#* [[w:en:Hávamál|Hávamál]] ([[s:en:Poetic Edda/Hávamál|English source]], [[s:is:Hávamál|Icelandic sourve]])
#*: ''Inn vari gestur''
#*: ''er til verðar kemur''
#*: ''[[þegja þunnu hljóði|þunnu hljóði þegir]],''
#*: ''eyrum hlýðir,''
#*: ''en '''augum''' skoðar.''
#*: ''Svo nýsist fróðra hver fyrir.''
#*:: The knowing guest
#*:: who goes to the feast,
#*:: In silent attention sits;
#*:: With his ears he hears,
#*:: with his '''eyes''' he watches,
#*:: Thus wary are wise men all.

